<asp:Content ContentPlaceHolderID="MainContent" runat="server">

    <p id='Extract'>So I'm on Maui and I can do anything I want. I hadn't been to the top
        of Haleakal&amacr; in years&mdash;for some reason I never went when
        living here during the pandemic&mdash;but today is the day! There's
        nothing between me and the summit but 10,000 feet!</p>

    <img src="20240806_000000.jpg" />

    <p>The ride from Olowalu to the top of Mount Haleakal&amacr; is a
        scenic and exhilarating journey. Starting from the coastal area of
        Olowalu, I drove through lush landscapes and charming towns like
        Lahaina and Kula. As you ascend, the road winds through diverse
        ecosystems, from tropical forests to arid volcanic terrain. The
        higher you go, the cooler the temperature becomes, and the views
        become more breathtaking. The final stretch to the summit of
        Haleakal&amacr;, which stands at over 10,000 feet, offers
        panoramic vistas of the island and beyond&mdash;sometimes.</p>

    <p>As we drive across the isthmus of Maui, primarily along the Maui
        Veterans Highway, the huge bulk of the volcano begins to rise up.</p>

    <img src='20240806_081238.jpg' />

    <p>As we drive along the Maui Veterans Highway, the three smokestacks of
        the Maalaea Power Plant come into view, standing tall against the
        backdrop of the bay. This facility, built in 1971, has been a
        significant source of electricity for Maui. The plant primarily uses
        fossil fuels to generate power, and its industrial presence is a
        stark contrast to the natural beauty surrounding it. As we pass by,
        we cant help but marvel at the juxtaposition of human engineering and
        the islands pristine landscapes. The sight of the smokestacks serves
        as a reminder of the islands journey towards balancing energy needs
        with environmental preservation. All new power needs are provided by
        wind and solar, of course.</p>

    <img src='20240806_081251.jpg' />

    <p>Closer to Haleakala, and as the morning evolved (the below
        photo was taken about 9:30 AM), the layer of clouds dutifully
        obscures the peak, as it does pretty much every day.</p>

    <img src='20240806_093833.jpg' />

    <p>Now on Haleakala Highway, but still on the isthmus, I passed
        extraordinarily tidy farmland on both sides. As I recall, one side
        was orange trees and the other was pineapples.</p>

    <img src='20240806_093934.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_094052.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_095110.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_100502.jpg' />

    <p>&hellip;gaining altitude with every switchback&hellip;</p>

    <img src='20240806_100956.jpg' />

    <p>Finally, I reached the actual entrance to Haleakala National Park.
        And, clever me, I had remembered to bring my Lifetime Senior Pass
        with me, so I didn't have to pay a thing. (Otherwise, it would be $30
        per vehicle.)</p>

    <img src='20240806_102107.jpg' />

    <p>Once entering the park we find a variety of native grasses. Some of
        the prominent species include Deschampsia nubigena, commonly known as
        Hawaiian bentgrass, and Eragrostis atropioides, also known as
        Hawaiian lovegrass. These grasses thrive in the sub-alpine shrubland
        zone. The temperature was about 60&deg;&hellip;a lot colder
        than it was yesterday at the beach!</p>

    <img src='20240806_102307.jpg' />

    <p>There's also a diverse array of native shrubs, ferns, and small woody
        trees. Some of the dominant plant species in this sub-alpine
        shrubland zone include p&umacr;kiawe (Leptecophylla tameiameiae),
        &omacr;helo (Vaccinium reticulatum), nohoanu (Geranium cuneatum),
        and aalii (Dodonaea viscosa). These plants create a lush tapestry of
        greenery that covers the mountain, providing habitat for various
        endemic birds such as the apapane, amakihi, and
        n&emacr;n&emacr;.</p>

    <img src='20240806_103617.jpg' />

    <p>And now, we look <i>down</i> at those clouds! I've been
        here overnight, and that's the only time those clouds are absent
        (which gives a life-changing view of the lights below).</p>

    <img src='20240806_103637.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_104511.jpg' />

    <p>As we ascend to the summit of Haleakal&amacr;, the landscape transforms into
        a surreal, otherworldly terrain. At over 10,000 feet, we reach the
        Haleakal&amacr; Observatory, a premier site for astronomical research. Here,
        the towering structures of the Daniel K. Inouye Solar Telescope and
        the Pan-STARRS telescopes stand against the clear, blue sky. These
        advanced instruments, including the worlds largest solar telescope,
        offer unparalleled views of the cosmos and contribute to significant
        scientific discoveries. The observatorys remote location and high
        altitude provide ideal conditions for observing the heavens. However,
        the presence of telescopes on Haleakal&amacr; has sparked controversy, as
        the land is considered sacred by Native Hawaiians, leading to ongoing
        debates about the balance between scientific advancement and cultural
        preservation.</p>

    <img src='20240806_105643.jpg' />

    <p>The Haleakal&amacr; Visitor Center, located at an elevation of 9,740 feet,
        is a small stone building perched at the edge of a cliff. This center
        has been welcoming visitors since 1936 and was constructed by the
        Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC). The center provides restrooms,
        water, and ranger services, offering visitors a chance to learn about
        the unique geological and ecological features of the summit
        area.</p>

    <p>Probably because this was a weekday, the
        parking lot at the summit wasn't very full. I gratefully hobbled to
        the bathroom, went through the visitor center&mdash;nothing new
        there&mdash;then got some shots of the faux caldera.</p>

    <img src='20240806_110426.jpg' />

    <p>At the top, we find what many refer to as the crater, but this
        impressive depression is not a true caldera. Unlike a caldera, which
        forms when the summit of a volcano collapses after an eruption,
        Haleakals crater was shaped by the forces of erosion. Over millennia,
        the headwalls of two large erosional valleys merged at the summit,
        creating the vast and dramatic depression we see today.</p>

    <img src='20240806_110427.jpg' />

    <p>This erosional valley took shape after the rimrock lava flows erupted
        around the top of the volcano approximately 145,000 years ago, give
        or take about 10,000 years.</p>

    <img src='20240806_110428.jpg' />

    <p>In January 1965, the Apollo 11 astronauts, including Neil Armstrong,
        Buzz Aldrin, and Michael Collins, trained extensively on the ground
        in Hawaii. The unique volcanic landscape of Mount Haleakal&amacr; provided
        an ideal environment for simulating the conditions they would
        encounter on the lunar surface. The training involved collecting
        geological specimens, practicing site selection, and documenting
        geological characteristics, which helped the astronauts prepare for
        their historic mission to the moon.</p>

    <img src='20240806_110753.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_110823.jpg' />

    <p>At the summit of Mount Haleakal&amacr;, the rare and striking Haleakal&amacr;
        Silversword (Argyroxiphium sandwicense subsp. macrocephalum) stands
        resilient. With its silvery-green leaves and blueish tips, this spiky
        marvel thrives in the harsh alpine desert, a testament to nature's
        adaptability and beauty.</p>

    <img src='20240806_111545.jpg' />

    <p>I would love to have made the walk to the actual summit (10,023 feet
        above sea level) and even started out, but between my hip, months of
        reduced activity, and the altitude-thin air, I just couldn't make it.
        But that's okay; I've been there before!</p>

    <img src='20240806_111708.jpg' />

    <p>And now for our descent! Down from the sub arctic to the land of
        flowering trees, and the warm waters of the Pacific.</p>

    <img src='20240806_111747.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_112930.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_115224.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_151421.jpg' />

    <p>I made a quick stop at my beloved Papalaua Beach Park (they have a
        Porta-Potty) and grabbed a couple shots before returning to my
        campground.</p>

    <img src='20240806_153456.jpg' />
    <img src='20240806_153502.jpg' />

    <p>Surprisingly, I wasn't all that tired when I got to Camp Oluwalu. The
        jet-lagging must've been pretty much over. So I chilled at the picnic
        table, enjoying the twilight.</p>

    <img src='20240806_193236.jpg' />

    <p>And when I woke (as usual) in the middle of the night, after the moon had set,
        again I was treated to a festival of stars unlike anything you can
        see in Phoenix. Even with a streetlight illuminating the tree, they
        were brilliant. Plus, I'm not sure, but there's been a lot of extreme
        solar activity lately, and this almost looks like an aurora photo.</p>

    <img src='20240806_202248.jpg' />

</asp:Content>
